course: dictionary
lesson: 7372
title: Plenty
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Plenty means a lot, or more than enough. Often it comes before «of», as in plenty of room, plenty of time or plenty of water. «Don't bundle up under blankets to sweat it out: light clothes, a cool room and plenty to drink are best.» Here plenty to drink means enough drink, and more than enough is fine.
explain: Plenty can also stand on its own, in place of a noun, as in «There is plenty, so take what you need.» In informal speech it can even be used like an adverb, as in «That's plenty big enough.» Look at the words next to plenty to see which job it is doing.
explain: A good check is to swap in «a lot of» or «enough». In «giving the bear plenty of room», plenty of room means a lot of room, so the sentence still makes sense. That is how you know you have understood it.
fact: Plenty means a lot, or more than enough.
fact: «Plenty of» is used before a noun to mean a lot of, such as plenty of time or plenty of water.
fact: Plenty can also mean a time of having lots of food and goods, as in a land of plenty.
fact: Useful phrases with plenty are plenty more, plenty to eat, plenty of time and in plenty.
fact: «When there is plenty of something and few people want it, its price usually falls.»
exercise: «Light clothes, a cool room and plenty to drink are best.» What does plenty mean here? | Hot tea with lemon | Only a little, just a sip | A cold kind of drink | *A lot, or more than enough
exercise: What does «plenty of» do before a noun? | It means nothing, so you can skip it | It means not many at all | *It means a lot of, as in plenty of time | It turns the noun into a verb
exercise: In a story, what does a land of plenty have? | Nothing to eat at all | *Lots of food and goods | Strange animals only | Cold, wet weather every day
test: «He stays next to his dad, they talk in calm voices and slowly back away, giving the bear plenty of room.» What does plenty of room mean? | *A lot of space | A room with a bed in it | Not much space at all | Room only for one person
test: Which phrase means you can have a lot more of something? | glum more | barely more | *plenty more | curt more
exam: Which sentence uses plenty on its own, as a pronoun? | He drank plenty of water | *There is plenty, so take what you need | Plenty of cats sleep all day | We saw plenty of trees
